{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And she could no longer.</b> Rashi explains: the Egyptians counted for her from the day she was returned, and she gave birth at six months and one day, and they investigated after him at the end of nine months. And it is difficult from what we say in Sotah: the Egyptians counted for her from the day she was returned, and she was already three months pregnant from the outset — which implies he was born at nine months. And one may answer: certainly he was born at six months; but to the Egyptians too it was known that one who gives birth at seven months gives birth at the completion [of seven]; and they counted that she conceived from the day she was returned, and immediately investigated after her — and she was already three months pregnant before the [latter] taking, two [earlier]. And therefore she hid him for three months." "<b>A reed-ark (תיבת גומא).</b> Because it resembles the reeds that are on the bank of the Nile, so the enemies would not recognize it. In Sotah they conclude from here: for the righteous, their property is more precious to them than their bodies — and why so much? Because they do not stretch out their hand to robbery." "<b>And she daubed it with clay (חמר).</b> And on the outside with clay. And on the outside, so that they would not recognize it — for many times the reeds (גמי) and the rushes that are on the bank of the Nile are somewhat sullied with mud; but if it had been pitch (זפת) on the outside, they would see the blackness on the outside and recognize it immediately. However, Rashi explained the opposite: the clay on the inside and the pitch on the outside. And R. Avraham ibn Ezra explained 'and she daubed it with clay and with pitch' after the manner of 'the roaring of a lion and the voice of a fierce lion' — even though תחמר is not applicable to pitch, it is mentioned along with clay, just as נתעו is mentioned alongside שאגה and קול — even though it is not applicable, because it is at least applicable to 'two young lions' [where it does fit]."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The phrase 'הלקוחים שניים' is obscure; translated hyper-literally as 'the [latter] taking, two.' The passage appears to distinguish between two stages of investigation or seizure by the Egyptians, but the syntax is difficult."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:33.661550673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}